Why this is not decided here
----------------------------

The choice changes who can adopt the tool, and it interacts with the point of
the product. agent-safety-gate exists to be the artefact you show an auditor or
a customer. A licence that stops a team from running it inside their own
pipeline is itself a barrier to entry - the same kind of barrier the rest of
this repository works to remove. That trade-off is a business decision, not an
engineering one.

The three realistic options, with consequences, are set out in
docs/OWNER_DECISIONS.md. Summary:

  * Apache-2.0        - widest adoption, explicit patent grant, permits closed
                        commercial forks. Best if the goal is that gate records
                        become something other people's tooling reads.
  * BSL 1.1           - source-available, free for internal use, blocks a
                        competitor from selling it as a service, converts to an
                        open licence on a date you set. Slows some enterprise
                        adoption because legal review is unfamiliar with it.
  * Proprietary       - matches the aos-kernel notice and keeps every option
    demonstrator        open, but evaluators cannot run it in a real pipeline,
                        which removes most of the value of the five-minute
                        quickstart.

Whatever is chosen, this file, the `license` field in pyproject.toml and the
licence line in both READMEs must be updated together.
